WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.569
Uh, let's see.

00:00:01.569 --> 00:00:06.660
Let's go next to, uh, let's go to our caller
from the six oh five area code.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.379
Who's calling today from six oh five.

00:00:08.379 --> 00:00:09.379
All right.

00:00:09.379 --> 00:00:10.379
Hi David.

00:00:10.379 --> 00:00:12.060
Can you hear me?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.060
Yeah.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.060
Who's this?

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.060
Okay.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.060
Okay.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.060
This is Daniel from South Dakota.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.370
Hey Daniel, what's going on?

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:24.680
So I support a living, you know, $15 an hour
wage, but some people like Andrew Yang has

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.570
been speaking a lot about automation recently.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:31.360
And so I feel that like a legitimate concern
that some people have about a living wage

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.370
is that in order to save money, companies
can just automate away some of their jobs.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.890
Uh, and you know, they'll continue to make
a profit because of that.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.250
And that's already been happening like in
Fast Food I've noticed, uh, with automated

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:47.590
kiosks and stuff, she's, the question is,
is acceleration of automation, is that like

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.470
a legitimate concern at all when it comes
to a living wage?

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.900
And if so was ubi and effective way to counter
that?

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.969
So listen, I mean here, so the, the answer
is yes, automation is a concern when we think

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:03.560
about living wages, but it's not one that
is particularly exacerbated by the winimum

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.969
wage being $15 versus 13.

00:01:05.969 --> 00:01:09.749
And you know a lot of people like to point
to the fast food kiosks as you talk about

00:01:09.749 --> 00:01:16.070
because after minimum wages were increased
in certain places, you saw fast food locations

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.090
there deploy these kiosks.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.770
Those kiosks have been in development for
a very long time.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:25.009
It doesn't just happen overnight that the
minimum wage goes up and then all of a sudden

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:26.389
these kiosks show up.

00:01:26.389 --> 00:01:30.359
So the truth is that those companies had been
planning on doing that for a very long time.

00:01:30.359 --> 00:01:34.719
And so I, if your argument, and it sounds
like at least part of your argument is that

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:39.049
a $15 minimum wage is not going to solve the
problem of technological automation.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:42.859
I agree with you, and this is one of the big
sort of missing elements that I'm glad Andrew

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:47.349
Yang is talking about it and many, many other
elected officials are not.

00:01:47.349 --> 00:01:52.789
It's that whether the minimum wage is 1315
or 17 number one in lots of places that's

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:55.670
not a living wage or it's barely a living
wage.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:56.670
That's a real issue.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.969
And number two, the issue of automation as
well as machine learning and artificial intelligence

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:05.989
is going to make a lot of that minimum wage
discussion, not obsolete, but it's going to

00:02:05.989 --> 00:02:08.390
make it less applicable to a lot of jobs.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:09.560
So I'm completely with you.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.780
It's not being discussed in a serious manner.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.240
So would you support having both the ubi and
a living wage?

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:18.240
Yeah.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.730
Listen, I'm in support of all of those things.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:24.700
And for people who criticize just a $15 minimum
wage as being merely a bandaid on our economic

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.320
system, I agree, right?

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.989
There's a bigger problem that had bandaids
not going to fix ubi of $1,000 no matter.

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:36.320
You know, I disagreed with Andrew Yang's initial
version, which was the thousand replaces other

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.320
benefits.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:43.200
I think it should be additive, but that disagreement
aside, that stuff only is on the fringes of

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.460
the broader economic problems, or I guess
I would call them deeper economic problems

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.290
that we have to solve.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.000
All right.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Thank you.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Appreciate the phone call.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Thank you very much.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Great.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:56.006
Uh, great to hear from you

